{
"segments": [
{
"text": "Case Nine.",
"start": 0.75,
"end": 1.774,
"pause": 0.6
},
{
"text": "The Sunset over the Sea.",
"start": 2.374,
"end": 3.931,
"pause": 1.4
},
{
"text": "Tidewhistle Cove faces east. Every morning, Agnes Bell opens the shutters of the Kettle and Gull and watches the sun climb up out of the sea, straight in front of her. In the evening, the sun goes down behind the hills at the back of the village, and the sea turns soft and grey.",
"start": 5.331,
"end": 22.611,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Demelza Rowe painted watercolours of the harbour and sold them from a little stall on the sea front. Her best one, a painting of the lighthouse at dawn, was on its easel at seven in the evening when she popped home for her supper. When she came back at nine, it was gone.",
"start": 23.461,
"end": 39.909,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Agnes and Constable Ollie Penrose spoke to three people who had been near the sea front that evening.",
"start": 40.759,
"end": 46.839,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Tamsin Trevelyan had been running the book club in her shop from seven until nine, and eight people said she had not left once.",
"start": 47.689,
"end": 54.943,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Hedley Truscott had been playing darts at the inn all evening. The whole darts team vouched for him, and he had lost, loudly, every game.",
"start": 55.793,
"end": 64.411,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "The third was Mr Ashdown, a visiting painter who had admired Demelza's work out loud more than once.",
"start": 65.261,
"end": 72.024,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "\"I wasn't on the sea front at all,\" said Mr Ashdown. \"I was up on the clifftop with my sketchbook from seven until well after nine. I sat and painted the sun setting into the sea. It was the most beautiful thing I have ever seen. The sea turned gold as the sun sank right down into it.\"",
"start": 72.874,
"end": 91.071,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Agnes, who had watched the sun come up out of that same sea that very morning, said nothing for a while.",
"start": 91.921,
"end": 97.575,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Can you solve it? What is wrong with Mr Ashdown's story?",
"start": 98.675,
"end": 101.853,
"pause": 1.2
},
{
"text": "Think about it.",
"start": 103.053,
"end": 104.077,
"pause": 0.9
},
{
"text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
"start": 104.977,
"end": 110.204,
"pause": 4.5
},
{
"text": "The Solution.",
"start": 114.704,
"end": 115.728,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Mr Ashdown took the painting.",
"start": 116.828,
"end": 118.748,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Tidewhistle Cove faces east. The sun rises out of the sea in front of the village, and in the evening it sets behind the hills at the back. Nobody in Tidewhistle Cove could have watched the sun setting into the sea, because the sea is on the wrong side. Mr Ashdown had invented his evening on the clifftop.",
"start": 119.598,
"end": 139.054,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Tamsin had been with her book club and Hedley had been with the darts team. Mr Ashdown was the one person with no true account of his evening.",
"start": 139.904,
"end": 148.523,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "The painting was found in his room at the inn, carefully wrapped. He confessed that he had wanted to study how Demelza painted her dawn light, because he had never managed it himself. Demelza let him buy the painting properly, at full price, and gave him a free lesson on sunrises. She suggested that he get up early to watch one.",
"start": 149.373,
"end": 169.895,
"pause": 0.0
}
],
